End-Stage COPD
Palliative & End of Life
Severe resting dyspnea, Accessory muscle use, Pursed-lip breathing

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PEL-025
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe exacerbation of resting dyspnea and profound fatigue preventing the patient from transferring to the commode.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported the patient was suffering from severe, unremitting dyspnea at rest with profound systemic fatigue, rendering them completely unable to mobilize or clear thick secretions. The patient's severe respiratory distress, coupled with signs of right-sided heart failure and sheer exhaustion, mandated an urgent after-hours medical assessment to optimize immediate symptom control, manage right-sided fluid overload, and prevent an imminent respiratory arrest or hospital admission.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I can't push the air out. I'm too weak to cough up this phlegm, and my legs are completely swollen."
History of Present Illness: The patient suffers from severe, continuous dyspnea strictly occurring with minimal physical activity and completely at rest. There is a chronic, highly productive cough generating copious thick sputum, which the patient is now too weak to expectorate. The spouse reports a prolonged, highly labored expiratory phase with distinct audible wheezing. The patient exhibits profound systemic fatigue and progressive, severe weight loss despite the peripheral edema. There are recurrent, severe acute exacerbations necessitating frequent emergent hospitalizations in the past 6 months. Tonight, the dyspnea worsened significantly, accompanied by signs of right-sided heart failure including visibly elevated jugular venous pressure and peripheral edema.
Functional Status: Acutely bedbound due to severe dyspnea and fatigue; normally ambulates short distances with a frame.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of secretion retention causing a fatal mucus plug; severe caregiver exhaustion from continuous monitoring.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/85 mmHg | HR: 112 bpm | Temp: 37.0 C | RR: 28 bpm | SpO2: 86% on room air | Wt: 72 Kg (with gross peripheral edema)
General: Cachectic patient with a barrel-shaped chest, visibly exhausted, sitting fully upright, and utilizing pursed-lip breathing.
Physical Exam: Respiratory exam reveals obligate use of accessory respiratory muscles at rest. Chest is barrel-shaped clinically generating hyperresonant percussion notes. Auscultation reveals a prolonged expiratory phase with widespread polyphonic wheezes and coarse crackles at the bases indicating retained secretions. Cardiovascular exam confirms right-sided heart failure signs including visibly elevated jugular venous pressure (JVP +5cm) and 3+ pitting peripheral edema to the mid-thighs. The diagnostic tetrad is confirmed by the observed severe resting dyspnea, massive accessory muscle use, and the instinctive pursed-lip breathing utilized to auto-peep.
Environmental Observations: Nebulizer machine is at the bedside but the patient is too fatigued to hold the mask properly.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient has been taking oral steroids and nebulized bronchodilators without symptomatic relief tonight. Diuretics have not been optimized for the right heart failure.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: End-Stage COPD - Status: Exacerbated (Cor Pulmonale / Secretion Retention)
Differentials:

Acute Left Ventricular Failure (Pulmonary Edema) - Ruled out/unlikely due to the distinctly hyperresonant chest, prolonged expiratory wheeze, and history of severe COPD driving isolated right-sided failure, rather than primary left-sided failure.

Acute pneumonia - Ruled out/unlikely as the primary acute driver due to the absence of fever, though underlying chronic airway colonization is present.

Pneumothorax - Ruled out/unlikely due to the presence of bilateral (though diminished) breath sounds and bilateral wheeze, lacking a sudden unilateral absence of sound.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, critical exacerbation of end-stage COPD, characterized by severe resting dyspnea, cor pulmonale (right-sided heart failure), and profound fatigue leading to secretion retention. The objective findings of hypoxia, massive peripheral edema, and exhaustion confirm the patient is failing to cope. Because the patient is entirely physically exhausted and oral medications are failing, delaying assessment until normal hours would inevitably result in fatal hypercapnic respiratory failure or a massive mucus plug. Immediate after-hours intervention was vital to administer parenteral diuretics to offload the right heart, provide low-dose parenteral opioids to relieve the work of breathing, and arrange urgent functional support.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered stat intravenous furosemide to rapidly initiate diuresis and offload the severe right-sided fluid overload. Administered a very small dose of stat subcutaneous morphine (2.5mg) specifically to relieve the central perception of dyspnea and reduce the terrifying work of breathing without suppressing the respiratory drive.
Positive Behavioral Change: When you feel the need to cough (trigger), you must firmly hold a small pillow tightly against your stomach and perform a "huff" maneuver instead of a hard cough (behavior). This supports your diaphragm and uses less energy, allowing you to successfully clear the thick phlegm without completely exhausting yourself (positive consequence).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral to community palliative care for symptom management and daily weight monitoring.
DME/Safety (Optional): Implement a bedside commode and a mechanical bed to maintain strict upright positioning.
Follow-up: GP review in 24 hours to titrate oral loop diuretics and monitor renal function.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the patient becomes completely unresponsive, develops severe new chest pain, or if the breathing stops completely, the spouse must administer the prescribed crisis subcutaneous medications and contact the palliative care hotline or 000 immediately, depending on the documented Advanced Care Directive goals.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The lung damage is irreversible; the focus is exclusively on reducing the sensation of breathlessness and offloading the fluid placing strain on the heart.
Clinical Course: The patient will likely remain heavily dependent on medications to manage the resting breathlessness. Severe exacerbations will become more frequent.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Spouse: Do not attempt to force the patient to lie flat. Continue to use the nebulizer as charted, but assist the patient with the mask.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The severe lung disease has caused the right side of your heart to struggle, leading to the massive fluid build-up in your legs and worsening your breathing. The IV injection will quickly force your kidneys to pee out that extra fluid, taking the strain off your heart. The small pain injection will ease the terrifying feeling of air hunger.
Actionable Steps: Stay completely upright. Use the huff coughing technique with the pillow. Do not try to walk to the bathroom; use the commode.

